.\"	ve.1: the 286 port's own
.\"
.TH VE 1 "November 20, 2026"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
ve \- screen editor in the manner of vim
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ve
[
.B \-R
] [
.BI + line
] [ file ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ve
is a screen editor with the modes and the most used commands of
.IR vim .
It is for a terminal of any size, and a file of any length up to 16
megabytes.
Only so much of the text is in memory as the
.B cache
option allows, in segments of its own
.RI ( segalloc (2));
the rest is in a file in /tmp, which a file that fits in the cache
never needs.
.PP
A file is shown as soon as enough of it has been read to fill the
window.
The rest is read while nothing is typed, and a command that moves over
what is there is done at once; a search reads as far as it goes; any
other command waits for the rest, and ^C ends the wait.
.PP
With
.B \-R
the files are not to be written.
.BI + line
puts the cursor on that line of the first file, and
.B +
alone on its last.
.PP
A line may be 2000 characters; a longer one is cut in two when it is
read.
A line longer than the screen is wide is not folded: the window
moves sideways to keep the cursor in sight.
Null characters are left out of a file as it is read.
.SH "NORMAL MODE"
A command may have a count before it, and
.BI \(dq x
to name the register, a to z, that it takes text from or keeps it in.
.PP
Motions:
.nf
.ta 2.2i
h j k l, the arrows	left, down, up, right
0 ^ $ Home End	start of line, first character, end
w b e W B E	words, and words between blanks
f\fIc\fP F\fIc\fP t\fIc\fP T\fIc\fP ; ,	to a character in the line
gg G \fIn\fPG	first line, last, line \fIn\fP
H M L	top, middle, bottom of the window
{ }	paragraphs
%	the matching bracket; \fIn\fP% is \fIn\fP percent
/\fIpat\fP ?\fIpat\fP n N * #	searches
\(aa\fIx\fP \(ga\fIx\fP \(aa\(aa	marks; where the cursor last jumped from
+ \- Return _ |	lines, and a column
.fi
.PP
Operators, each followed by a motion, by itself for whole lines, or by
an object:
.B d
delete,
.B c
change,
.B y
yank,
.B >
and
.B <
shift,
.B !
filter through a command.
The objects are
.B iw aw iW aW
a word,
.B "i( a( i[ a[ i{ a{ i< a<"
brackets and what is in them,
.B "i\(dq a\(dq i\(aa a\(aa"
quotes, and
.B "ip ap"
a paragraph.
.PP
Other commands:
.nf
i a I A o O	insert: before, after, at the start, the end, on a new line
R	replace mode
x X D C s S Y	short for dl dh d$ c$ cl cc yy
p P	put the register after, or before
r\fIc\fP ~ J	replace a character, change case, join lines
u ^R .	undo, redo, repeat the last change
v V	visual mode, by characters or lines
m\fIx\fP	mark the place \fIx\fP, a to z
^F ^B ^D ^U ^E ^Y, PgUp PgDn	scroll
zt z. zb	put the cursor's line at the top, middle, bottom
^G ^L ^Z	say where; draw again; stop for a while
ZZ ZQ	write and quit; quit without
& ^^	repeat the last :s; edit the file before this one
.fi
.PP
The last 40 changes can be undone.
In visual mode the motions move the end of the area, and
.B "d x y c s < > ! J ~ u U r :"
work on it;
.B o
goes to its other end.
.SH "INSERT MODE"
What is typed goes into the file until ESC.
Backspace takes out what is before the cursor, the end of the line
before included;
.B ^W
a word and
.B ^U
the line so far.
.B ^T
and
.B ^D
shift the line,
.B ^V
puts in the next key whatever it is, and the arrows, Home, End, PgUp,
PgDn and Delete do what they say.
Insert changes between inserting and replacing.
.SH "COMMAND LINE"
After a colon come the commands of
.IR ex ,
with their lines before them: a number,
.B .
the cursor's line,
.B $
the last,
.BI \(aa x
a mark,
.BI / pat /
and
.BI ? pat ?
a search, each with
.BI + n
or
.BI \- n
perhaps, and
.B %
for all of them.
.nf
.ta 2.2i
w[!] [\fIfile\fP], w >> \fIfile\fP	write
wq, x, q[!]	write and quit, the same if changed, quit
e[!] [\fIfile\fP], e #	edit a file; the one before this
r \fIfile\fP, r !\fIcommand\fP	read in a file, or what a command writes
n[!], prev, rew, args	the files named on the command line
f [\fIname\fP]	say what file, or name it
d [\fIx\fP], y [\fIx\fP], pu [\fIx\fP]	delete, yank, put
m \fIline\fP, t \fIline\fP	move and copy
j, >, <	join, shift
s/\fIpat\fP/\fInew\fP/[g], &	substitute, and again
g/\fIpat\fP/\fIcommand\fP, v/\fIpat\fP/\fIcommand\fP	for every line with, or without
!\fIcommand\fP, \fIlines\fP!\fIcommand\fP, sh	the shell; filter lines
p, nu, =	show lines, with numbers, a line's number
k\fIx\fP, u, red	mark, undo, redo
se \fIoption\fP	set
so \fIfile\fP, cd \fIdir\fP, h	commands from a file, change directory, this page
.fi
.PP
In
.I new
.B &
is what the pattern matched,
.BR \e1 " to " \e9
its parts, and
.B \en
ends a line.
In a command for the shell
.B %
is the file's name and
.B #
that of the file before it.
.PP
A pattern has
.B ". [ ] * ^ $ \e< \e> \e( \e)"
as in
.IR ed (1),
and
.B "\e+ \e? \es \eS \ed \ew \et"
besides.
.PP
The options, with their short names:
.B autoindent
(ai),
.BI cache= n
(128), the memory for text in K, from 32 to 960,
.B expandtab
(et),
.B ignorecase
(ic),
.B list,
.B number
(nu),
.BI shiftwidth= n
(sw, 8),
.BI tabstop= n
(ts, 8), and
.B wrapscan
(ws, set).
One is unset with
.B no
before its name.
.SH FILES
.ta 1.5i
$HOME/.verc	commands done at the start, a line each
.br
\fIfile\fP.save	what was being edited when the terminal hung up
.br
/tmp/ve*	the text that is not in memory; it has no name once it is open
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ex(1), vi(1), ed(1), segalloc(2)
.SH BUGS
When /tmp is full a file is read only as far as there was room, and
is not to be written; a change is still made, but may be one that
cannot be undone.
There is one file at a time, one window, no folding of long lines,
no maps or macros, and no alternation in patterns.
A count before
.B o
or
.B O
is not used.
